closed	p2	SSE personal-overlay phase holds a pooled DB connection across its yields (main.py stream_updates, the 'if not scoped and stream_user ...' block ~:717-810): it opens 'async with _pool.connection() as db_conn' then yields SSE frames to the client INSIDE that block. While suspended at a yield the connection is checked out; if the client disconnects mid-overlay the generator is cancelled while a query is in flight, returning an ACTIVE connection to the pool -> 'another command is already in progress' on the pool's reset-rollback -> connection discarded as BAD. This is the deeper root cause of the pool-churn seen in #179 (there it was amplified by the bot's 1-2/s reconnect storm; the storm is fixed, but a browser disconnecting mid-overlay still churns one connection). FIX: adopt the shape of _scoped_catchup_frames (added in #179) - buffer all overlay rows into a list under the connection, release the connection, THEN yield. The catch-up phase (stream_objects_new) already releases before yielding; only the overlay holds across yields. Pre-existing (not introduced by #179); flagged by principal-engineer review of #179. relates:#179 relates:#178
